P2: The per-credential lock that is meant to prevent concurrent OAuth2 token fetches (and the 'recheck cache after acquiring lock' stampede guard) is silently defeated by the cleanup in this finally block. When the async with _get_lock(...) block exits, the lock is released but a queued waiter hasn't been scheduled to run yet, so lock.locked() is False and the code pops the lock out of _locks. A new caller then creates a fresh lock and proceeds in parallel with the still-waiting task, so both fetch tokens concurrently and each writes to the cache (last write wins). Recommend not removing the lock from the dict while a waiter could still be queued (e.g. leave the lock in place, or track waiter counts) so single-flight is preserved.

